Derive the following Carnot Efficiency and Thermodynamic Temperature relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\frac{Q_H}{Q_C}
=\frac{nRT_H\ln(V_B/V_A)}{nRT_C\ln(V_C/V_D)}
=\frac{T_H}{T_C}.
\tag*{[3]}
\]
Notation: \(T_H,T_C\) denotes absolute temperatures of the hot and cold reservoirs, with T_H\gt T_C; unit \(\mathrm{K, K}\); \(Q_H\) denotes positive heat magnitude absorbed from the hot reservoir; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(Q_C\) denotes positive heat magnitude rejected to the cold reservoir; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(W_{\mathrm{out}}\) denotes positive net work delivered by the engine; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(\eta_{\mathrm C}\) denotes Carnot heat-engine efficiency; dimensionless; \(n\) denotes amount of ideal gas used to demonstrate the result; unit \(\mathrm{mol}\); \(R\) denotes molar gas constant; unit \(\mathrm{J mol^{-1} K^{-1}}\); \(V_A,V_B,V_C,V_D\) denotes ideal-gas volumes at successive Carnot states; unit \(\mathrm{m^{3}, m^{3}, m^{3}, m^{3}}\).
